<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Random walk and diffusive models are ubiquitous in mathematics, physics,
biology and social sciences, in particular when the random walker
moves through the vertices and edges of a graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\left(V,E\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\left(V,E\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
In this scenario a random walker at the vertex <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> at
time <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can move to any of the nearest neighbors of <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with equal
probability at time <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. That is, if as
illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>(a) the vertex <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> has three nearest
neighbors <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{k,l,m\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{k,l,m\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> the random walker can move to
any of them with probability <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{jm}=p_{jl}=p_{jk}=k_{j}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{jm}=p_{jl}=p_{jk}=k_{j}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree of <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. We can figure out situations in which
the movement of the random walker at a given position is facilitated
by the large degree of any of its nearest neighbors.
Here we use a relaxed definition of the term “hub”. Although this term is used in network theory for those nodes of exceptionally large degree, we use it here in the following way: If there are two connected vertices of different degree, we call a “hub” the one of larger degree. This is formally defined later on in the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then, let us suppose that there are situations in which the probability
that the random walker moves to a nearest neighbor of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> at time
<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> increases with the degree of the nearest neighbor. This is
illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>(b) where <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}&gt;k_{l}&gt;k_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}&gt;k_{l}&gt;k_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
consequently <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{jm}&lt;p_{jl}&lt;p_{j{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{jm}&lt;p_{jl}&lt;p_{j{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will refer hereafter to this
scenario as the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“hubs-attracting</span>” one. Another possibility
is that the random walker is repelled by large degree vertices, such as
for <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}&gt;k_{l}&gt;k_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}&gt;k_{l}&gt;k_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{jm}&gt;p_{jl}&gt;p_{j{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{jm}&gt;p_{jl}&gt;p_{j{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> as illustrated
in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>(c). We will refer to this model as the “<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-repelling</span>”
one. These scenarios could be relevant in the context of real-world
complex networks where vertices represent the entities of the system
and the edges the interrelation between them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
An example of hubs-repelling strategies of navigation are some of
the diffusive processes in the brain where there is a high energetic
cost for navigating through the hubs of the system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.
Hubs-attracting mechanisms could be exhibited, for instance, by diffusive
epidemic processes in which hubs are major attractors of the disease and can be considered at high risk of contagion by spreaders in the network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
