schemes of a particle hopping on a network.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From a mathematical perspective, one of the most important aspects
of the model of random walks on graphs is its connection with the
graph Laplacian matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>
and with the concept of resistance distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
In a simple graph the resistance distance is the effective resistance
between two vertices <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, which measures the resistance of
the total system when a voltage is connected across <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>.
Klein and Randić <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> proved that the effective
resistance is a squared Euclidean distance between the two vertices of
the graph, which can be obtained from the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse
of the graph Laplacian. It is also known that the commute time between
two vertices <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> of a random walker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>,
i.e., the number of steps of a random walker starting at <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, before
arriving at <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and returning to <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> again, is proportional to the
resistance distance between the two vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Strategies for avoiding large/small degree vertices in random-walk processes
on graphs/networks have been proposed in the literature under the
general umbrella of ‘degree-biased random walks’ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
However, in the current work we go beyond the random walk formulation
of the problem and express it in terms of hubs-biased Laplacians <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>
and the corresponding resistance distance matrices on graphs. Thus,
we focus here on the algebraic properties of these resistance distance
matrices. We note in passing that the current concept has nothing
in common with the so-called “degree-resistance” between a pair
of vertices, which is nothing else that the resistance distance multiplied
by the difference of degrees of the vertices forming the corresponding
edge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the relation between resistance distance and commute time of
random walks on graphs, we study here the efficiency of hubs attracting/repelling
diffusive processes on graphs. In closing, in this work we
define the hubs-biased resistance distances between pairs of vertices
of a simple graph and study their main spectral properties. We also
propose analogues of the Kirchhoff index <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>,
the semi-sum of all resistance distances in the graph, for the hubs-biased
resistances. We report here several bounds for the two new kinds of
resistance distances as well as for the corresponding Kirchhoff indices.

Finally, we study the commute time of the hubs-attracting random walks and analyze their relative improvement over the standard one.
We observe that certain classes of real-world networks, such as brain/neuronal networks and electronic circuits, have normal random walks as efficient as the hubs-attracting one, while others, like infrastructural networks,
can reduce their average commuting times by 300% by using the hubs-attracting
mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this article we consider simple weighted graphs.
We will always impose the following throughout.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assum">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumptions 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a weighted graph with vertex set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and edge set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. The underlying unweighted graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is simple and finite: we denote by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the number of vertices and edges, respectively. To avoid trivialities, we also assume <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and that no vertex is isolated. We also denote by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> its number of connected components.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Additionally, each edge is assigned a weight by means of a surjective mapping <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:E\rightarrow W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:E\rightarrow W</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the following we use interchangeably the terms graphs and networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be the adjacency matrix of the (weighted) graph <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and
let <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the degree of the vertex <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in V</annotation></semantics></math>,

i.e., the sum
of the <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th row or column of <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>; or equivalently <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}:=\#\mathcal{N_{\mathit{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}:=\#\mathcal{N_{\mathit{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N_{\mathit{j}}}=\left\{j\in V|\left(i,j\right)\in E\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N_{\mathit{j}}}=\left\{j\in V|\left(i,j\right)\in E\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all nearest neighbors of <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will denote the minimal and maximum degree by <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varDelta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varDelta</annotation></semantics></math>,
respectively. Let <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> be a node such that <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in{\mathcal{N_{\mathit{i}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in{\mathcal{N_{\mathit{i}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we say that <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is a “hub”, more correctly a “local hub”, if it has the largest degree among all <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in{\mathcal{N_{\mathit{i}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in{\mathcal{N_{\mathit{i}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will denote by <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> the diagonal matrix of vertex degrees.
<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          In the case of weighted graphs
the degree is often referred as <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strength</span>, but we will use the general term degree here in all cases.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following condensed notation across this paper.
If <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a number depending on an index <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> – in
the following typically <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> – then we
will write <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> to symbolize both <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
depending on the choice on the index <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> be the finite-dimensional Hilbert space of functions on <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the inner product</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,g\rangle=\sum_{v\in V}f(v)\overline{g(v)},\qquad f,g\in\ell^{2}(V)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,g\rangle=\sum_{v\in V}f(v)\overline{g(v)},\qquad f,g\in\ell^{2}(V).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The standard graph Laplacian is an operator in <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl(\mathscr{L}f\bigr)(v)\coloneqq\sum_{w\in V:\,(v,w)\in E}{\varphi(vw)}\bigl(f(v)-f(w)\bigr),\qquad f\in\ell^{2}(V)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>f</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\mathscr{L}f\bigr)(v)\coloneqq\sum_{w\in V:\,(v,w)\in E}{\varphi(vw)}\bigl(f(v)-f(w)\bigr),\qquad f\in\ell^{2}(V),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(v,w)\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(v,w)\in W</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight of the edge <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,w)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,w)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in the following <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{1_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{1_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the all-ones column vector of order <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> all-one matrix; and <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the identity matrix of order <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we introduce the concepts of hubs-biased Laplacians in the context
of resistive networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conductance</span> function is a function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:V\times V\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:V\times V\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>
which respects adjacency between vertices, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(v,w\right)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(v,w\right)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\left(v,w\right)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\left(v,w\right)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">total conductance</span> at a vertex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}c\left(v\right)&amp;\coloneqq\sum_{\left(v,w\right)\in E}c\left(v,w\right)\\
&amp;=\sum_{w\in V}c\left(v,w\right).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}c\left(v\right)&amp;\coloneqq\sum_{\left(v,w\right)\in E}c\left(v,w\right)\\
&amp;=\sum_{w\in V}c\left(v,w\right).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(The second equality in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Definition 3.2. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) holds in view of Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To motivate the following definition, let us now consider a diffusive particle that does not necessarily hop to any nearest neighbor with the same probability. More precisely, in a <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-attracting model</span> the hopping of a diffusive particle from a vertex <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to a target neighboring vertex <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is favored by a (comparatively) small degree of <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and a large degree of <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> (“<math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is a hub”), corresponding to a small ratio <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{k_{i}}{k_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{k_{i}}{k_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, the hopping from <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is disfavored by a (comparatively) large degree of <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and a (comparatively) small degree of <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that these conditions for the hopping from <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are different from the ones for the hopping from <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>: hubs-attracting diffusion is <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> symmetric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specularly, <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-repelling</span> models are conceivable in which the hopping from <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> would be favored by a comparatively large degree of <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and a comparatively small degree of <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, and disfavored by a comparatively small degree of <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and a comparatively large degree of <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We will capture this intuition by the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph satisfying the Assumptions <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumptions 2.1. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-biased Laplacian</span> corresponding to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is the operator
on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> defined by</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}f\bigr)(v)\coloneqq\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)\bigl(f(v)-f(w)\bigr),\qquad f\in\ell^{2}(V)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}f\bigr)(v)\coloneqq\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)\bigl(f(v)-f(w)\bigr),\qquad f\in\ell^{2}(V),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where here and in the following</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right):=\left(\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}\right)^{\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right):=\left(\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}\right)^{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We call <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-repelling Laplacian</span> if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>; or <span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-attracting Laplacian</span> if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\dfrac{k_{w}}{k_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\dfrac{k_{w}}{k_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Actually, we could easily extend this definition by allowing for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>; for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the unweighted
case, we would then recover the standard (discrete) Laplacian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, we will not explore this direction in this paper.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to understand the main difference between the current approach and some previous approaches introduced for the analysis of weighted directed graphs we should point out the following. First, we analyze here unweighted and undirected graphs, which are then transformed into a very specific kind of weighted directed graphs. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, among others, the authors focus on weighted directed graphs. Then, they generate either asymmetric Laplacians <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{a}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>
or symmetrized versions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> thereof <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Yet another approach was proposed by Young et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> where the resistance distances are calculated from a matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=2Q^{T}{\Sigma}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=2Q^{T}{\Sigma}Q</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathscr{L}}{\Sigma}+{\Sigma}\tilde{\mathscr{L}}^{T}=I_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathscr{L}}{\Sigma}+{\Sigma}\tilde{\mathscr{L}}^{T}=I_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathscr{L}}=QLQ^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathscr{L}}=QLQ^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> obeys the following conditions: <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q1_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q1_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QQ^{T}=I_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QQ^{T}=I_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{T}Q=I_{n}-(1/n)J_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{T}Q=I_{n}-(1/n)J_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathscr{L}}=Q{\mathscr{L}}Q^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathscr{L}}=Q{\mathscr{L}}Q^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is known as the reduced Laplacian. In a further work, Fitch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> demonstrated that the resistance distances obtained through the matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for any pair of vertices in any connected, directed graph, are equal to the resistance distances obtained for a certain symmetric, undirected Laplacian on the same set of nodes and possibly admitting negative edge weights. As the hubs-biased Laplacian matrices are non-symmetric in general, it is obvious that these two approaches are not equivalent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the current study a different kind of matricial structure emerges, which is neither the asymmetric cases previously considered nor a symmetric one. The matrices <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the class of quasi-reciprocal matrices (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>), which has not been previously used in the analysis of graphs. An <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quasi-reciprocal</span> if</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}\neq 0\Rightarrow M_{ji}=M_{ij}^{-1},\forall i,j=1,2,\ldots,n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}\neq 0\Rightarrow M_{ji}=M_{ij}^{-1},\forall i,j=1,2,\ldots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, due to this notable difference the approach developed here for the resistance distance and related descriptions of undirected graphs are substantially different from the ones previously analyzed for weighted directed graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> be a standard orthonormal basis in <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>
consisting of the vectors</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{v}(w)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if $w=v$},\\[2.15277pt]
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{v}(w)\coloneqq\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if $w=v$},\\[2.15277pt]
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then, <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the
vectors <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}e_{v})(w)=\begin{cases}c_{\alpha}(v)&amp;\text{if $w=v$},\\[2.15277pt]
-c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)&amp;\text{if $(v,w)\in E$},\\[2.15277pt]
0&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}e_{v})(w)=\begin{cases}c_{\alpha}(v)&amp;\text{if $w=v$},\\[2.15277pt]
-c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)&amp;\text{if $(v,w)\in E$},\\[2.15277pt]
0&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\alpha}(v)=\sum_{w\in\mathscr{\mathcal{N_{\mathit{v}}}}}c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\alpha}(v)=\sum_{w\in\mathscr{\mathcal{N_{\mathit{v}}}}}c_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">is the total <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-conductance of the vertex <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the hubs-biased
matrices can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}=\Xi_{\alpha}-K^{\alpha}AK^{-\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}=\Xi_{\alpha}-K^{\alpha}AK^{-\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.4" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\Xi_{\alpha}&amp;:=\diag\left(c_{\alpha}(v)\right)_{v\in V},\\
K&amp;:=\diag\left(k_{v}\right)_{v\in V},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>K</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\Xi_{\alpha}&amp;:=\diag\left(c_{\alpha}(v)\right)_{v\in V},\\
K&amp;:=\diag\left(k_{v}\right)_{v\in V},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.5" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the unweighted adjacency matrix of the graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let us note an elementary but important fact concerning the trace of (any) hubs-biased Laplacians.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There holds</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trhb\left(G\right):=\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{1}\right)=\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{-1}\right)=\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>vw</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trhb\left(G\right):=\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{1}\right)=\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{-1}\right)=\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us, for <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the
definition of total conductance and in view of (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Definition 3.2. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>),</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}c_{\alpha}\left(v\right)=\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\left(\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}\right)^{\alpha}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}c_{\alpha}\left(v\right)=\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\left(\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}\right)^{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, because <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{v,w}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{v,w}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{w,v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{w,v}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (and in this case both addends <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}},\dfrac{k_{w}}{k_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}},\dfrac{k_{w}}{k_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>
appear), we conclude that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}c_{\alpha}\left(v\right)=\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}:" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{tr}\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}c_{\alpha}\left(v\right)=\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}:</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">since the right-hand side is independent of <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the claim is proved.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A rather natural generalization of Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3.3. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> involves infinite graphs. Let for a moment – unlike under the standing Assumptions <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumptions 2.1. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>! – the graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be allowed to have infinitely many vertices. If the degree sequence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> is still bounded, then by (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Definition 3.3. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>) the sequence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{\alpha}(v))_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{\alpha}(v))_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, too; it is then easy to see that the matrices <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\pm\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\pm\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> define bounded linear operators on the (infinite dimensional!) space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that for such <span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">infinite, uniformly locally bounded graphs</span> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined as a bounded linear operator on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, too. However, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> have absolutely continuous spectrum, rather than a discrete set of eigenvalues as in the finite case: for this reason, this setting is not convenient for our purposes: for example, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> will not have finite trace.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The double-sided bound</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m\dfrac{\delta}{\varDelta}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq 2m\dfrac{\varDelta}{\delta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>Δ</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m\dfrac{\delta}{\varDelta}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq 2m\dfrac{\varDelta}{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">on the hubs-biased trace immediately follows from (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>), since <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq k_{v}\leq\varDelta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq k_{v}\leq\varDelta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>,
and because <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}=\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}=2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}=\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}=2m</annotation></semantics></math> by the Handshaking
Lemma. (We recall that <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varDelta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varDelta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the minimum and maximum degree of the
vertices of <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of edges of <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The estimates in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>) are rough, yet sharp as both inequalities become equalities for complete graphs. (We should observe that if <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is complete, then
<math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trhb\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trhb\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math> also agrees with the trace of the standard discrete Laplacian.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We are able to provide a few improved estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There holds</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{1}{\varDelta}\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\dfrac{1}{\delta}\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{1}{\varDelta}\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\dfrac{1}{\delta}\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The bounds follow directly from (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>), since</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in V}a_{vw}k_{v}=k_{v}\sum_{w\in V}a_{vw}=k_{v}^{2}\qquad\hbox{for all $v\in V$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in V}a_{vw}k_{v}=k_{v}\sum_{w\in V}a_{vw}=k_{v}^{2}\qquad\hbox{for all $v\in V$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">Summing over <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> yields the claimed bounds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Because of the well-known identity</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}=\sum_{(v,w)\in E}\left(k_{v}+k_{w}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}=\sum_{(v,w)\in E}\left(k_{v}+k_{w}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p15.2" class="ltx_p">the estimates in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>) imply those in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>),
but are clearly sharper if, e.g., <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is bi-regular (recall that <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bi-regular</span> if <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned in <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{v}\equiv k_{1}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{v}\equiv k_{1}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S3.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{w}\equiv k_{2}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{w}\equiv k_{2}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">More explicit bounds in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Lemma 3.8. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>) can be obtained using
known estimates on <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the so-called (first) <span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Zagreb index</span> and <span id="S3.p16.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Randić index</span> of <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively: we refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> for a comprehensive survey of results on this topic, including improving bounds for special classes, like planar or triangle-free graphs, that might be of interest in applications. In particular, we mention the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There holds</span></p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{4m^{2}}{n\varDelta}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\dfrac{2m\left(2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\right)}{\delta\left(n+\varDelta-\delta\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{4m^{2}}{n\varDelta}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\dfrac{2m\left(2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\right)}{\delta\left(n+\varDelta-\delta\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The lower estimate becomes an equality if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is regular. The upper estimate becomes an equality if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-t</annotation></semantics></math> vertices forming an independent set.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The claimed bounds follow from the known estimates</span></p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{4m^{2}}{n}\leq\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}\leq\dfrac{2m\left(2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\right)}{n+\varDelta-\delta}:" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{4m^{2}}{n}\leq\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}^{2}\leq\dfrac{2m\left(2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\right)}{n+\varDelta-\delta}:</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Remark 4 and Remark 5]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, where also the extremal graphs are characterized.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) The lower estimate in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a>) is significantly
better than the lower estimate in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>):
indeed, the net effect is like replacing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> by the average
degree <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{2m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{2m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>). The upper
estimate in (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a>) is better than the upper estimate
in (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>) if and only if</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\leq\varDelta\left(n+\varDelta-\delta\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\leq\varDelta\left(n+\varDelta-\delta\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">which is sometimes the case, for instance for any regular graph, and
sometimes not, for instance for any path on more than 2 edges. The
equality in (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In Remark 3.10. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.18</span></a>) holds for complete graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2)
Further estimates on the hubs-biased trace may be obtained re-writing (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>) in alternative ways, including</p>
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}=\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}\left(\sum_{w\in V}\dfrac{a_{vw}}{k_{w}}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}\left(\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{1}{k_{w}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}=\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}\left(\sum_{w\in V}\dfrac{a_{vw}}{k_{w}}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}k_{v}\left(\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{1}{k_{w}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.2" class="ltx_p">or</p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}=\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{1}{k_{v}}\left(\sum_{w\in V}a_{vw}k_{w}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{1}{k_{v}}\left(\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v,w\in V}a_{vw}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}=\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{1}{k_{v}}\left(\sum_{w\in V}a_{vw}k_{w}\right)=\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{1}{k_{v}}\left(\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.3" class="ltx_p">(we recall that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of nearest neighbors of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>).
Considering the minima <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> and maxima <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the degree function in neighborhoods <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> we can deduce from (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In Remark 3.10. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a>)
and (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In Remark 3.10. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.20</span></a>) the following sharper estimates:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\delta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\delta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V}\delta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\sum_{v\in V}\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V}\delta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}\leq\trhb\left(G\right)\leq\sum_{v\in V}\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p5.1" class="ltx_p">which shows, for instance, that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trhb\left(G\right)=2pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trhb\left(G\right)=2pq</annotation></semantics></math>
for the complete bipartite graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p,q}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p,q}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p6.1" class="ltx_p">(3) Invoking Titu’s Lemma we also obtain</p>
<table id="S3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{1}{k_{w}}\geq\dfrac{\left(\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}1\right)^{2}}{\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}}=\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{1}{k_{w}}\geq\dfrac{\left(\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}1\right)^{2}}{\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}}=\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Because Titu’s Lemma is equivalent to the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,
the previous inequality becomes an equality if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(1\right)_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(1\right)_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(k_{w}\right)_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(k_{w}\right)_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly dependent,
i.e., if and only if the degree function is constant on each neighborhood, which
is the case for instance in regular and bi-regular graphs. We finally deduce the estimate</p>
<table id="S3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trhb\left(G\right)\geq\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{k_{v}^{3}}{\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trhb\left(G\right)\geq\sum_{v\in V}\dfrac{k_{v}^{3}}{\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}k_{w}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p7.2" class="ltx_p">which of course implies the lower estimate in (<a href="#S3.E21" title="In Remark 3.10. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now collect a few important properties of the hubs-biased Laplacian matrices of any graphs satisfying our standing Assumptions <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumptions 2.1. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the hubs-biased Laplacian matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
enjoys the following properties:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i) its eigenvalues are real;</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii) it is positive semidefinite;</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(iii) <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{{rank\,}}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=n-\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>rank </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{{rank\,}}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=n-\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math>;</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(iv) it can be diagonalized as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)\Lambda_{\alpha}\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)\Lambda_{\alpha}\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{\alpha}-A=U_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}U_{\alpha}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{\alpha}-A=U_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}U_{\alpha}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is as in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">(i) Under the Assumptions <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumptions 2.1. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, no vertex is isolated, hence <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, for any <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> the diagonal matrix <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible and we observe that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}&amp;=\Xi_{\alpha}-K^{\alpha}AK^{-\alpha}\\
&amp;=K^{\alpha}\left(K^{-\alpha}\Xi_{\alpha}K^{\alpha}-A\right)K^{-\alpha}\\
&amp;=K^{\alpha}\left(\Xi_{\alpha}-A\right)K^{-\alpha},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}&amp;=\Xi_{\alpha}-K^{\alpha}AK^{-\alpha}\\
&amp;=K^{\alpha}\left(K^{-\alpha}\Xi_{\alpha}K^{\alpha}-A\right)K^{-\alpha}\\
&amp;=K^{\alpha}\left(\Xi_{\alpha}-A\right)K^{-\alpha},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">since <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is diagonal, too. We conclude that
<math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
is similar to the symmetric matrix <math id="S3.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\Xi_{\alpha}-A\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\Xi_{\alpha}-A\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so their eigenvalues are real.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">(ii) Now let <math id="S3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\ell^{2}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\ell^{2}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,
we can write</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}x^{T}\left(\Xi_{\alpha}-A\right)x&amp;=\sum_{\left(i,j\right)\in E}\left(\left(k_{i}^{\alpha/2}k_{j}^{-\alpha/2}\right)x_{i}-\left(k_{j}^{\alpha/2}k_{i}^{-\alpha/2}\right)x_{j}\right)^{2}\end{split}\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}x^{T}\left(\Xi_{\alpha}-A\right)x&amp;=\sum_{\left(i,j\right)\in E}\left(\left(k_{i}^{\alpha/2}k_{j}^{-\alpha/2}\right)x_{i}-\left(k_{j}^{\alpha/2}k_{i}^{-\alpha/2}\right)x_{j}\right)^{2}\end{split}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, because <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{\alpha}-A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{\alpha}-A</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are similar, we have that <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x}^{T}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}{x}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x}^{T}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}{x}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">(iii)
Let us now prove that the dimension of the null space of <math id="S3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="S3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector such that <math id="S3.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}{z}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}{z}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that for every <math id="S3.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(i,j\right)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(i,j\right)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}=z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}=z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore <math id="S3.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{z}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the same value on all vertices of the same
connected component, which indicates that the dimension of the null
space is <math id="S3.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S3.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{{rank\,}}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=n-\mathscr{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>rank </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{{rank\,}}{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=n-\mathscr{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">(iv) Finally, we also have that because <math id="S3.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{\alpha}-A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{\alpha}-A</annotation></semantics></math>
is symmetric we can write it as: <math id="S3.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{\alpha}-A=U_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}U_{\alpha}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{\alpha}-A=U_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}U_{\alpha}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S3.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)\Lambda_{\alpha}\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}=\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)\Lambda_{\alpha}\left(KU_{\alpha}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
which indicates that all hubs-biased Laplacians are diagonalizable.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p24.1" class="ltx_p">We know from Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 3.11. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> that all eigenvalues of any hubs-biased Laplacian are real positive: we will denote them by <math id="S3.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,1}\ldots,\rho_{\alpha_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,1}\ldots,\rho_{\alpha_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> in ascending order, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=\rho_{\alpha,1}\leq\ldots\leq\rho_{\alpha,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=\rho_{\alpha,1}\leq\ldots\leq\rho_{\alpha,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p24.2" class="ltx_p">Let us conclude this section deducing some estimates on them. To avoid trivialities, and in view of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 3.11. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>.(iii), in the remainder of this section we always assume the graph <math id="S3.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to be connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\left\{-1,1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,n}\geq\frac{4m^{2}}{n(n-1)\varDelta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,n}\geq\frac{4m^{2}}{n(n-1)\varDelta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left.\begin{array}[]{c}\dfrac{2m\left(2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\right)}{\delta(n-1)\left(n+\varDelta-\delta\right)}\\[15.0pt]
\dfrac{2m\varDelta}{(n-1)\delta}\end{array}\right\}\geq\rho_{\alpha,2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left.\begin{array}[]{c}\dfrac{2m\left(2m+\left(n-1\right)\left(\varDelta-\delta\right)\right)}{\delta(n-1)\left(n+\varDelta-\delta\right)}\\[15.0pt]
\dfrac{2m\varDelta}{(n-1)\delta}\end{array}\right\}\geq\rho_{\alpha,2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p25.1" class="ltx_p">We note in passing that on a <math id="S3.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(k+1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(k+1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>-star, the upper bounds in (<a href="#S3.E28" title="In Corollary 3.12. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.28</span></a>) reduce to</p>
<table id="S3.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left.\begin{array}[]{c}2+k\\
2k+1\end{array}\right\}\geq\rho_{\alpha,2,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left.\begin{array}[]{c}2+k\\
2k+1\end{array}\right\}\geq\rho_{\alpha,2,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p25.2" class="ltx_p">which does not prevent <math id="S3.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,2}</annotation></semantics></math>
to tend to <math id="S3.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as the graph grows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p26.1" class="ltx_p">We also remark that these estimates are only really interesting for
non-regular graphs, because in regular graphs the hubs-biased Laplacians
coincide with the standard discrete Laplacian for which many different
bounds are known for its eigenvalues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that <math id="S3.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{1}{n-1}\trhb\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{1}{n-1}\trhb\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math> yields
the arithmetic mean of all non-zero eigenvalues of <math id="S3.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>;
in particular, the lowest non-zero eigenvalue <math id="S3.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,2}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot
be larger than <math id="S3.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{1}{n-1}\trhb\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{1}{n-1}\trhb\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
while the largest eigenvalue <math id="S3.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,n}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be larger that
<math id="S3.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{1}{n-1}\trhb\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{1}{n-1}\trhb\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking into account
(<a href="#S3.E13" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a>) we
deduce the claimed estimate.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) The naive upper bound</p>
<table id="S3.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,n}\leq 2\max_{v\in V}\left({\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}\right)_{vv}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,n}\leq 2\max_{v\in V}\left({\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}\right)_{vv}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.2" class="ltx_p">on the largest eigenvalue of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> follows
from Geršgorin’s Theorem, since by (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>) <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}\right)_{vv}=\sum_{w\in V}\left|\left({\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}\right)_{vw}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}\right)_{vv}=\sum_{w\in V}\left|\left({\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}\right)_{vw}\right|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now,</p>
<table id="S3.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\left({\mathscr{L}}_{-1}\right)_{vv}&amp;=\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{k_{w}}{k_{v}}\leq\dfrac{\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}}{k_{v}}\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}1=\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}\leq\varDelta,\\
\left({\mathscr{L}}_{1}\right)_{vv}&amp;=\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}\leq\dfrac{k_{v}}{\delta}\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}1=\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\delta}\leq\dfrac{\varDelta^{2}}{\delta},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\left({\mathscr{L}}_{-1}\right)_{vv}&amp;=\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{k_{w}}{k_{v}}\leq\dfrac{\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}}{k_{v}}\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}1=\varDelta_{\mathcal{N}_{v}}\leq\varDelta,\\
\left({\mathscr{L}}_{1}\right)_{vv}&amp;=\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{k_{v}}{k_{w}}\leq\dfrac{k_{v}}{\delta}\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}1=\dfrac{k_{v}^{2}}{\delta}\leq\dfrac{\varDelta^{2}}{\delta},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which are both sharp for regular graphs. Plugging these expressions in (<a href="#S3.E30" title="In Remark 3.13. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.30</span></a>) yields</p>
<table id="S3.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{-1,n}\leq 2\varDelta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{-1,n}\leq 2\varDelta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1,n}\leq 2\dfrac{\varDelta^{2}}{\delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1,n}\leq 2\dfrac{\varDelta^{2}}{\delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(2) In order to show some lower bounds on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we remark that the standard discrete
Laplacian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
share the null space, cf. the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 3.11. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>.(iii). Then, we can quotient it out, thus studying the lowest eigenvalue of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathscr{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathscr{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{L}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}^{n}/\left\langle{\mathbf{1}}\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}^{n}/\left\langle{\mathbf{1}}\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, the space of vectors orthogonal to the vector <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{1}}=(1,\ldots,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{1}}=(1,\ldots,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking a normalized vector <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in this space one sees that</p>
<table id="S3.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{\delta}{\varDelta}\left(\mathscr{L}f,f\right)\leq\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}f,f\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{\delta}{\varDelta}\left(\mathscr{L}f,f\right)\leq\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}f,f\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, choosing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to be an eigenfunction associated with
the lowest non-zero eigenvalue <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
and applying the Courant–Fischer min-max characterization of the eigenvalues of the Hermitian matrix we deduce</p>
<table id="S3.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{\delta}{\varDelta}\rho_{2}\leq\rho_{\alpha,2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{\delta}{\varDelta}\rho_{2}\leq\rho_{\alpha,2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the second lowest eigenvalue of the discrete
Laplacian, i.e., the algebraic connectivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Alternatively, for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> we can use</p>
<table id="S3.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\left(\mathscr{L}_{\textnormal{norm}}f,f\right)\leq\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}f,f\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\left(\mathscr{L}_{\textnormal{norm}}f,f\right)\leq\left(\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}f,f\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and deduce</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\rho_{2,\textnormal{norm}}\leq\rho_{\alpha,2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\rho_{2,\textnormal{norm}}\leq\rho_{\alpha,2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\textnormal{norm}}\coloneqq K^{-1/2}\mathscr{L}K^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>norm</mtext></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>K</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\textnormal{norm}}\coloneqq K^{-1/2}\mathscr{L}K^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the normalized Laplacian. Now we can either apply explicit formulae
for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and  <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2,\textnormal{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2,\textnormal{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for classes of graphs or use general estimates like</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}\geq 2\eta\left(1-\cos\dfrac{\pi}{n}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}\geq 2\eta\left(1-\cos\dfrac{\pi}{n}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p5.1" class="ltx_p">from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> or</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2,\textnormal{norm}}\geq\left\{\begin{array}[]{c}\dfrac{1}{Dn},\\
1-\cos\dfrac{\pi}{m},\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>norm</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2,\textnormal{norm}}\geq\left\{\begin{array}[]{c}\dfrac{1}{Dn},\\
1-\cos\dfrac{\pi}{m},\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p6.1" class="ltx_p">from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, respectively, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>
is the edge connectivity and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the diameter of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p7.1" class="ltx_p">(3) Further sharp bounds on the Zagreb index are known, including <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem 2.3 and Theorem 2.6]</cite>, immediately yielding</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varDelta+\frac{(2m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)}+\frac{2(n-2)(\varDelta_{2}-\delta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)^{2}}\leq\trhb(G)\leq\frac{(n+1)m-\varDelta(n-\varDelta)}{\delta}+\frac{2(m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\delta(n-2)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>HB</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varDelta+\frac{(2m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)}+\frac{2(n-2)(\varDelta_{2}-\delta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)^{2}}\leq\trhb(G)\leq\frac{(n+1)m-\varDelta(n-\varDelta)}{\delta}+\frac{2(m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\delta(n-2)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varDelta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varDelta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the second largest degree of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the upper bound holding under the additional assumption that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. (A characterization of the extremal graphs where equality is attained is available, too, but is rather technical.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Following the same proof as in Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Corollary 3.12. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a> finally yields the bounds</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha,n}\geq\frac{\varDelta}{n-1}+\frac{(2m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)^{2}}+\frac{2(n-2)(\varDelta_{2}-\delta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)^{3}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha,n}\geq\frac{\varDelta}{n-1}+\frac{(2m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)^{2}}+\frac{2(n-2)(\varDelta_{2}-\delta)^{2}}{\varDelta(n-1)^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p8.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{(n+1)m-\varDelta(n-\varDelta)}{\delta(n-1)}+\frac{2(m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\delta(n-2)(n-1)}\geq\rho_{\alpha,2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{(n+1)m-\varDelta(n-\varDelta)}{\delta(n-1)}+\frac{2(m-\varDelta)^{2}}{\delta(n-2)(n-1)}\geq\rho_{\alpha,2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Hubs-biased Resistance Distance</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adapt here some general definitions of resistive networks to the
case of hubs-biased systems, mainly following the classic formulations
given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
Let us
consider <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as a resistive network in which every edge <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(v,w\right)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(v,w\right)\in E</annotation></semantics></math>
has edge resistance <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)\coloneqq c_{\alpha}^{-1}\left(v,w\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)\coloneqq c_{\alpha}^{-1}\left(v,w\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the connection of a voltage between the vertices <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\left(v,w\right)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\left(v,w\right)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be the net current out the
source <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and into the sink <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\left(v,w\right)=-i\left(w,v\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\left(v,w\right)=-i\left(w,v\right).</annotation></semantics></math>
Then, according to the first Kirchhoff’s law we have that <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}i\left(v,w\right)=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}i\left(v,w\right)=I</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a source, <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}i\left(v,w\right)=-I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}i\left(v,w\right)=-I</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink, or zero otherwise, where we have denoted by <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> the net current flowing through the whole network. The application of the second
Kirchhoff’s law, namely that <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\left(v,w\right)\in C}i\left(v,w\right)r_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\left(v,w\right)\in C}i\left(v,w\right)r_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=0</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle with edges labeled in consecutive order, implies
that a potential <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{V}</annotation></semantics></math> may be associated with any vertex
<math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for all edges</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\left(v,w\right)r_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\left(v,w\right)r_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">which represents the Ohm’s law, and where <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{V}</annotation></semantics></math>
depend on the net current <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and on the pair of vertices where the
voltage source has been placed. Let us now define formally the <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-biased effective resistance</span>, which is the resistance
of the total system when a voltage source is connected across a corresponding
pair of vertices. Throughout this section we are still adopting the notation in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: in particular, <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hubs-biased effective resistance</span> between the vertices <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right):=\dfrac{\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)}{I}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>I</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right):=\dfrac{\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)}{I}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove the following result, showing that hubs-biased resistance between any two vertices of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a squared Euclidean distance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,w\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,w\in V</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> the hubs-biased resistance <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,v\right)+\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,v\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,v\right)+\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,v\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse
of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we will prove that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,v\right)\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,v\right)\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{e}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{e}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector with all entries equal to zero
except the one corresponding to vertex <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> which is equal to one.
Using the second Kirchhoff’s law we have</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{1}{r\left(v,w\right)}\left(\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)\right)=\begin{cases}I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a source},\\
-I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a sink},\\
0&amp;\hbox{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>v</mi><mtext> is a source</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>v</mi><mtext> is a sink</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w\in\mathcal{N}_{v}}\dfrac{1}{r\left(v,w\right)}\left(\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)\right)=\begin{cases}I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a source},\\
-I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a sink},\\
0&amp;\hbox{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">which can also be written as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\alpha}\left(v\right)\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\sum_{w=1}^{n}\dfrac{1}{r\left(v,w\right)}\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)=\begin{cases}I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a source},\\
-I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a sink},\\
0&amp;\hbox{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>v</mi><mtext> is a source</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>v</mi><mtext> is a sink</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\alpha}\left(v\right)\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\sum_{w=1}^{n}\dfrac{1}{r\left(v,w\right)}\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)=\begin{cases}I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a source},\\
-I\qquad&amp;\hbox{if $v$ is a sink},\\
0&amp;\hbox{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us write it in matrix-vector form as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}{V}}=I\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}{V}}=I\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Due to the fact that the right-hand side of (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 4 Hubs-biased Resistance Distance ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>) is orthogonal
to <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we can obtain <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathscr{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathscr{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)=\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{{V}}=I\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)=\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{{V}}=I\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then, using the definition of the effective resistance we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\dfrac{\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)}{I}=\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>I</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\alpha}\left(v,w\right)=\dfrac{\mathscr{V}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}\left(w\right)}{I}=\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now, because <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)=\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,v\right)+\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,v\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)^{T}\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left({e}_{v}-{e}_{w}\right)=\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,v\right)+\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(v,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,v\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
we only remain to prove that it is a distance. Let
<math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}=V_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}V_{\alpha}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}=V_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}V_{\alpha}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}=KU_{\alpha}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}=KU_{\alpha}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then
<math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}=V_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}V_{\alpha}^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}=V_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}V_{\alpha}^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> being the
Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse of the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues
of<math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math> i.e., the diagonal matrix whose <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th
entry is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}\left(i,i\right)=\begin{cases}0\quad&amp;\hbox{if the $i$th eigenvalue is $0$},\\
\rho_{\alpha,i}^{-1}\quad&amp;\hbox{if the $i$th eigenvalue is $\neq 0$}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if the </mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext>th eigenvalue is </mtext><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if the </mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext>th eigenvalue is </mtext><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}\left(i,i\right)=\begin{cases}0\quad&amp;\hbox{if the $i$th eigenvalue is $0$},\\
\rho_{\alpha,i}^{-1}\quad&amp;\hbox{if the $i$th eigenvalue is $\neq 0$}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us write the right-hand side of (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4 Hubs-biased Resistance Distance ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) as</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{v}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{u}_{\alpha}^{T}+{w}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}-{v}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}-{w}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{v}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{u}_{\alpha}^{T}+{w}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}-{v}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}-{w}_{\alpha}\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding rows of <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>
for the vertices <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Then, we have</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(u,u\right)+\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(u,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,u\right)\\
&amp;={v}_{\alpha}\left(\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T}-\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}\right)-{w}_{\alpha}\left(\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T}-\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}\right),\\
&amp;=\left({v}_{\alpha}-{w}_{\alpha}\right)\left(\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T}-\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}\right)\\
&amp;=\left({v}_{\alpha}-{w}_{\alpha}\right)\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}\left({v}_{\alpha}-{w}_{\alpha}\right)^{T}\\
&amp;=\left(\left({v}_{\alpha}-{w}_{\alpha}\right)\sqrt{\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}}\right)\left(\left({v}_{\alpha}-{w}_{\alpha}\right)\sqrt{\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}}\right)^{T}\\
&amp;=\left(\mathscr{V}_{\alpha}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}_{\alpha}\left(w\right)\right)^{T}\left(\mathscr{V}_{\alpha}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}_{\alpha}\left(w\right)\right)\\
&amp;=\left\|\mathscr{V}_{\alpha}\left(v\right)-\mathscr{V}_{\alpha}\left(w\right)\right\|^{2},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒱</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(u,u\right)+\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(u,w\right)-\mathscr{L}_{\alpha}^{+}\left(w,u\right)\\
&amp;={v}_{\alpha}\left(\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T}-\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}\right)-{w}_{\alpha}\left(\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{v}_{\alpha}^{T}-\Lambda_{\alpha}^{+}{w}_{\alpha}^{T}\right),\\
