<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The four graphs with the largest value of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathcal{\mathscr{R}}_{\mathnormal{\alpha=1}}}\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathscript" mathcolor="#000000">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathcal{\mathscr{R}}_{\mathnormal{\alpha=1}}}\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
among all connected graphs with 8 vertices are illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 4.1 ‣ 4.1 Hubs-biased Kirchhoff index ‣ 4 Hubs-biased Resistance Distance ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
