<div id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Again by Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem13" title="Remark 3.13. ‣ 3 Hubs-biased Laplacians and their spectra ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.13</span></a>,
<math id="S4.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\alpha=-1,n}\leq 2\varDelta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\alpha=-1,n}\leq 2\varDelta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{i}{\max}c_{\alpha=-1}\left(i\right)=\underset{i}{\max}k_{i}^{-1}\sum_{v\in\eta_{i}}k_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑖</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑖</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{i}{\max}c_{\alpha=-1}\left(i\right)=\underset{i}{\max}k_{i}^{-1}\sum_{v\in\eta_{i}}k_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the maximum being attained at those vertices <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with minimal degree <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and connected to <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> vertices <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
which all have the maximum degree <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varDelta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varDelta</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the result follows
using the Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Corollary 4.7. ‣ 4.1 Hubs-biased Kirchhoff index ‣ 4 Hubs-biased Resistance Distance ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The four graphs with the largest value of <math id="S4.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathcal{\mathscr{R}}_{\mathnormal{\alpha=-1}}}\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathcal{\mathscr{R}}_{\mathnormal{\alpha=-1}}}\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
among all connected graphs with 8 vertices are illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 4.2 ‣ 4.1 Hubs-biased Kirchhoff index ‣ 4 Hubs-biased Resistance Distance ‣ Hubs-biased resistance distances on graphs and networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2101.07103v2/Minimum.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/324;" width="381" height="324" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4.2: </span>Graphs with the maximum values of <math id="S4.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\mathcal{\mathscr{R}}_{\mathnormal{\alpha=-1}}}\left(G\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\mathcal{\mathscr{R}}_{\mathnormal{\alpha=-1}}}\left(G\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
